  overview: 'At 3,893 km² Murchison is the biggest protected area in Uganda, and the falls themselves are the most physically impressive thing in the country. The Nile arrives carrying the outflow of Lake Victoria, hits a narrow break in the rift escarpment and is compressed from a river hundreds of metres wide into a gap you could almost jump across. The resulting pressure — an estimated 300 cubic metres of water per second through that gap — is why it is sometimes described as the most powerful waterfall in the world by force rather than height.\n\nThe river divides the park in two, and the halves are quite different. North of the Nile the **Buligi circuit** runs across open borassus palm savanna and grassland, and this is where the game is: the park\'s Rothschild\'s giraffe population is the largest in the world, alongside elephant, buffalo, lion, leopard, Jackson\'s hartebeest and Uganda kob. South of the river is thicker woodland and scrub, quieter for game but the route to the top of the falls and through the Kaniyo Pabidi forest where chimpanzee tracking is offered.\n\nThe classic day combines a morning game drive on the north bank with an afternoon launch trip upstream to the base of the falls, and it works extremely well.',
  why_visit: 'It is the most complete safari in Uganda. You get genuine big-game viewing on open savanna, a boat trip that puts you in front of one of Africa\'s great waterfalls, hippo and crocodile at close range, chimpanzee tracking in Budongo forest, and the option to add Ziwa Rhino Sanctuary on the drive up to complete the Big Five. It is also considerably closer to Kampala than the western parks, which makes a three-day trip realistic.',
  getting_there: 'The drive from Kampala is 305 km to Masindi and the southern Kichumbanyobo gate, about 5 hours on good tarmac via Luweero and Nakasongola. For the northern Tangi and Chobe gates you continue via Karuma, roughly 6 hours. Almost everyone stops at Ziwa Rhino Sanctuary, which sits directly on the route about 180 km from Kampala.\n\nPublic transport gets you as far as Masindi comfortably — buses run from Kampala\'s Kisenyi terminal for UGX 20,000-30,000 — but there is no transport inside the park, so you would need to arrange a vehicle in Masindi. Aerolink flies Entebbe to Pakuba and Bugungu airstrips in about 1.5 hours.\n\nThe Paraa ferry links the two banks and is the pivot of most itineraries. It runs roughly hourly from 07:00 to 19:00; check current times with your lodge because the schedule does change.',
  travel_advice: 'Do the launch trip in the afternoon and the game drive at dawn — the light and the animal activity both favour that order. Book the launch through UWA at Paraa or via your lodge; the public boat is considerably cheaper than a private charter and perfectly good.\n\nThe short climb from the boat\'s turnaround point to the top of the falls is the best thing in the park and many visitors miss it. Arrange with your driver to be collected at the top of the falls rather than returning by boat. It is a steep 45-minute walk in full sun, so carry water.\n\nTsetse flies are present in the woodland south of the river. They are attracted to dark blue and black, so wear light colours. Malaria prophylaxis is strongly advised here.',
