  intro: 'Uganda\'s most visited savanna park, spread across the rift floor between two lakes with the Rwenzori glaciers on one horizon and volcanic craters on the other.',
  overview: 'Queen Elizabeth covers 1,978 km² of remarkably varied ground. The Kazinga Channel, a 32 km natural waterway, links Lake George to Lake Edward and runs right through the middle of the park — the boat trip along it delivers the densest concentration of hippo in Uganda plus buffalo, elephant and an extraordinary volume of birdlife. To the north the Kasenyi plains are open grassland where lion follow the Uganda kob breeding grounds. To the west the Katwe explosion craters give views back across the whole rift. And in the far south, separated by a two-hour drive, the **Ishasha sector** holds the tree-climbing lions the park is famous for.\n\nThose lions are genuinely unusual. Ishasha\'s prides habitually rest in the branches of large fig and acacia trees through the middle of the day — the leading explanations are relief from ground heat and biting flies, and a better vantage over the kob herds. Sightings are good but not guaranteed, and you need a morning or late afternoon drive with a ranger who knows the current tree.\n\nThe park also records 95 mammal species and around 600 birds, the highest bird list of any protected area in East Africa.',
  why_visit: 'It is the most accessible and most varied savanna experience in Uganda, and it sits on the natural route between Bwindi and Kibale, so almost every western itinerary passes through. The Kazinga Channel launch is outstanding value and requires no effort. Add tree-climbing lions, chimpanzees in the Kyambura Gorge, crater drives and the option of lion tracking with researchers, and there is easily enough for three days.',
  getting_there: 'From Kampala it is 410 km via Mbarara to the Katunguru gate, 6-7 hours on tarmac. Via Fort Portal it is 420 km and around 7 hours but a more scenic drive through the tea country and past Kibale. From Bwindi\'s Buhoma sector to Ishasha is only 2-3 hours, which is why the two combine so well.\n\nBuses from Kampala to Kasese or Mbarara pass the park gates and cost UGX 30,000-40,000; you can be dropped at Katunguru, but you will need a vehicle from there. Aerolink flies Entebbe to Mweya and Kasese airstrips in about 1.5 hours.\n\nGetting between the main northern sector and Ishasha means either a 2-3 hour drive around through Katunguru and Rukungiri, or the rough but shorter internal track — confirm its condition with rangers in the wet season.',
  travel_advice: 'If tree-climbing lions are your priority, sleep in or near Ishasha rather than at Mweya. The drive down is long enough that a day trip gives you only a narrow window in the wrong part of the day.\n\nThe standard Kazinga Channel launch leaves Mweya twice daily at around 11:00 and 15:00 and costs a fraction of a private charter. The 15:00 departure has better light and more animal activity.\n\nKyambura Gorge chimpanzee tracking is cheap but has a much lower success rate than Kibale — around 50-60% — because the community is small and the gorge is deep. Treat it as a bonus, not the reason you came.\n\nThe park is bisected by the public Kasese-Mbarara road, so you will pass through villages inside the boundary. Drive slowly through Katunguru and Katwe.',
